    "prompt": "Read the following passage carefully and answer the questions that follow.",
    "choices": null,
    "passage": "In the contemporary digital era, social media has emerged as a dominant force shaping the lives of young individuals. While it provides unprecedented opportunities for communication, self-expression, and access to information, its pervasive influence has raised serious concerns regarding youth mental health. The constant exposure to curated and idealized representations of life often leads to unhealthy comparisons, fostering feelings of inadequacy, low self-esteem, and anxiety among adolescents.\n\nMoreover, the addictive nature of social media platforms exacerbates these issues. The pursuit of validation through likes, comments, and followers can create a cycle of dependency, where self-worth becomes contingent upon virtual approval. This phenomenon not only distracts young people from real-life interactions but also contributes to social isolation and emotional distress. Studies have shown that excessive screen time is closely associated with sleep disturbances, reduced concentration, and increased levels of stress.\n\nAnother critical aspect is the prevalence of cyberbullying, which has become a significant threat in the digital landscape. Unlike traditional forms of bullying, online harassment can be relentless and far-reaching, leaving victims with little respite. The anonymity afforded by social media often emboldens perpetrators, intensifying the psychological impact on victims and, in severe cases, leading to depression and suicidal tendencies.\n\nHowever, it is important to acknowledge that social media is not inherently detrimental. When used responsibly, it can serve as a platform for awareness, support, and community building. Mental health campaigns, peer support groups, and access to professional resources have become more accessible through digital channels.\n\nIn conclusion, while social media offers numerous benefits, its adverse effects on youth mental health cannot be overlooked. A balanced approach involving digital literacy, parental guidance, and self-regulation is essential to mitigate its negative consequences and promote overall well-being.",
