    "guidance": "Model answer (3 marks, a short paragraph of four or five sentences): The passage suggests that human life and nature are quietly bound together, and that nature is a companion and a teacher rather than a mere setting. Grandfather Hari's memory of being lost in the forest shows this - he was lonely but not frightened, because the trees became his friends, and in the hills, as he says, one is never truly alone. The passage also sets human life against nature's longer time-scale: the seed planted in Binya's father's boyhood is now a young tree that will shade travellers long after both of them are gone, so nature outlives us and our care for it becomes a gift to people we will never meet. That thought is what makes the hills seem to Binya suddenly older and wiser than before. Structure your three marks as one idea each - companionship, continuity across generations, and the humility or wisdom nature teaches - and anchor each to a detail from the passage rather than writing general praise of nature."
